# Local variables

Both aliases assign in source order. Locals expand as individual arguments but
never enter the child environment. Local values shadow exported values only for
expansion, including sandbox defaults.

    :vars BIN=printf
    :b $BIN cat (available)
    :e SHADOW=environment
    :v FIRST=one SECOND=$FIRST-two LOCAL='two words' SHADOW=local HOME=local-home
    :$ $BIN '<%s>\n' ${SECOND} $LOCAL $SHADOW $HOME (exit: 0)

```stdout
<one-two>
<two words>
<local>
<local-home>
```

    :$ sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "$${LOCAL-missing}" "$$SHADOW"; test "$$HOME" != "$HOME" && echo home-not-exported' (exit: 0)

```stdout
<missing>
<environment>
home-not-exported
```

    :e SHADOW=changed DERIVED=$SHADOW
    :$ sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "$SHADOW" "$$SHADOW" "$$DERIVED"' (exit: 0)

```stdout
local
changed
local
```

    :e -u SHADOW
    :$ sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "$SHADOW" "$${SHADOW-missing}"' (exit: 0)

```stdout
local
missing
```

Names stay literal, including dollars. Values expand only once; reassignments
can refer to the old value. An empty local still shadows an exported value.

    :v NAME=EXPANDED $NAME=literal VALUE='$$FIRST' FIRST=$FIRST-again
    :$ printf '%s\n' '${$NAME}' $EXPANDED $VALUE $FIRST (exit: 0)

```stdout
literal

$FIRST
one-again
```

    :e EMPTY=environment
    :v EMPTY=
    :$ printf '<%s>\n' $EMPTY (exit: 0)

```stdout
<>
```

Paths and tmux arguments share expansion. Fenced file contents are not expanded.

    :v SOURCE=tests/cases/runner/text.md COPY='copy file.md' FILE='local file.txt'
    :cp $SOURCE $COPY (copied)
    :w $FILE (written)

```text
$FIRST stays literal
```

    :$ cat $FILE (exit: 0)

```stdout
$FIRST stays literal
```

Create a pane that prints `ready` and stays alive with `cat`. Expand its session
and pane targets, then send the local text. Durations and expectations remain
static. The snapshot should show `ready` followed by `variable text`.

    :v SESSION=local-session TARGET=local-session:0.0 TEXT='variable text'
    :t rename-session -t 0 runner (success)
    :t new-session -d -s $SESSION 'printf "ready\n"; cat' (success)
    :t resize-window -t $SESSION:0 -x 40 -y 4 (success)
    :p $TARGET
    :settle -d 2s (settled)
    :k $TEXT
    :snap -d 2s -e '(?m)variable text$'

```terminal
ready
variable text


```

The pane binding tracks the new selection; locals do not change child tmux
context. Successful pane selection replaces an explicit environment override.

    :$ sh -c 'test "$TMUX_PANE" = "$$TMUX_PANE" && echo pane-default-matches' (exit: 0)

```stdout
pane-default-matches
```

    :e TMUX_PANE=override
    :p runner:0.0
    :$ sh -c 'test "$TMUX_PANE" = "$$TMUX_PANE" && test "$$TMUX_PANE" != override && echo pane-selection-replaces-override' (exit: 0)

```stdout
pane-selection-replaces-override
```

Malformed assignments preserve earlier values and do not prevent later updates.

    :v INVALID =missing FIRST='${OPEN' LAST=$FIRST

> [!WARNING]
> invalid assignment 'INVALID': expected NAME=VALUE

> [!WARNING]
> invalid assignment '=missing': invalid variable name ''

> [!WARNING]
> invalid assignment 'FIRST=${OPEN': unterminated variable reference

    :$ printf '%s\n' $LAST (exit: 0)

```stdout
one-again
```

    :vars

> [!WARNING]
> Parser error: ':vars' expects at least one assignment

